### What is the Criteria API?

The Criteria API builds queries programmatically as a tree of objects instead of as a JPQL string. `CriteriaBuilder` is the factory that creates predicates and expressions. `CriteriaQuery<T>` represents the query itself. `Root<T>` represents the entity being queried. And each condition is a `Predicate` that you add to the WHERE clause.

This approach makes queries type-safe and ideal for dynamic construction, where the set of filters is not known until runtime.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.criteria.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountSearchService {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public AccountSearchService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }

    public List<Account> search(String accountType, BigDecimal minBalance, String status) {
        CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
        CriteriaQuery<Account> cq = cb.createQuery(Account.class);
        Root<Account> root = cq.from(Account.class);

        List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();

        if (accountType != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("accountType"), accountType));
        }
        if (minBalance != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("balance"), minBalance));
        }
        if (status != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("status"), status));
        }

        cq.where(cb.and(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0])));
        cq.orderBy(cb.desc(root.get("balance")));

        return em.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (accountType="SAVINGS", minBalance=50000, status=null):
[Account{SAV2001, Priya, 500000.00, SAVINGS},
 Account{SAV2003, Rajesh, 150000.00, SAVINGS}]
```

### What is Specification<T>?

`Specification<T>` is a Spring Data functional interface that wraps the Criteria API. Each specification contributes one predicate through its `toPredicate(root, query, criteriaBuilder)` method. You write small, reusable specifications per filter and compose them with `and()`, `or()`, and `not()`.

```java
package com.bank.spec;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountSpecs {

    public static Specification<Account> hasType(String type) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            type == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("accountType"), type);
    }

    public static Specification<Account> balanceAtLeast(BigDecimal min) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            min == null ? null : cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("balance"), min);
    }

    public static Specification<Account> hasStatus(String status) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            status == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("status"), status);
    }

    public static Specification<Account> holderNameContains(String name) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            name == null ? null : cb.like(
                cb.lower(root.get("holderName")),
                "%" + name.toLowerCase() + "%");
    }
}
```

Returning `null` from a specification when its input is absent means that filter is simply skipped. This is the elegant solution to optional filters.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.spec.AccountSpecs;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository repo;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    public List<Account> search(
            String type, BigDecimal minBalance, String status, String name) {

        Specification<Account> spec = Specification
            .where(AccountSpecs.hasType(type))
            .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(minBalance))
            .and(AccountSpecs.hasStatus(status))
            .and(AccountSpecs.holderNameContains(name));

        return repo.findAll(spec);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (type="SAVINGS", minBalance=null, status="ACTIVE", name=null):
[Account{SAV2001, Priya, 500000.00, SAVINGS, ACTIVE},
 Account{SAV2003, Rajesh, 150000.00, SAVINGS, ACTIVE}]
// minBalance and name filters were null, so they were skipped
```

The repository must extend `JpaSpecificationExecutor<Account>`:

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaSpecificationExecutor;

public interface AccountRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Account, String>,
                JpaSpecificationExecutor<Account> {
}
```

### Why do we need dynamic queries?

Fixed JPQL is fine when you know the exact query at compile time. But many real-world use cases require queries that change shape at runtime.

At National Bank of India, the account search screen has 8 optional filters. Priya might search by type and status, Rajesh might search by minimum balance only, and Pankaj might use all 8 filters together. With fixed JPQL, you would need every possible combination of filters as a separate query -- that is 2^8 = 256 queries.

With the Criteria API or specifications, you build one query dynamically by adding only the predicates that apply. No string concatenation, no fragment juggling, no risk of malformed queries.

```java
// The problem: building dynamic JPQL with string concatenation
// WRONG -- fragile, error-prone, injection-risky
public List<Account> search(String type, BigDecimal min) {
    StringBuilder jpql = new StringBuilder("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE 1=1");
    if (type != null) jpql.append(" AND a.accountType = '" + type + "'"); // INJECTION!
    if (min != null) jpql.append(" AND a.balance >= " + min);
    return em.createQuery(jpql.toString(), Account.class).getResultList();
}

// CORRECT -- use Criteria API or Specification
// No string concatenation, no injection risk
Specification<Account> spec = Specification
    .where(AccountSpecs.hasType(type))
    .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(min));
return repo.findAll(spec);
```

### How does the Criteria API work internally?

When you build a Criteria query, you are constructing a tree of objects that Hibernate translates into SQL. The `CriteriaBuilder` creates nodes in this tree: predicates for WHERE, expressions for SELECT, and orders for ORDER BY. When you call `em.createQuery(criteriaQuery)`, Hibernate walks the tree and generates the corresponding SQL for the configured database dialect.

```text
CriteriaQuery<Account>
  ├── SELECT: root (Account)
  ├── FROM: Account (root)
  ├── WHERE:
  │     AND
  │     ├── equal(accountType, "SAVINGS")
  │     └── ge(balance, 50000)
  └── ORDER BY: desc(balance)

Hibernate translates to:
  SELECT a.account_id, a.holder_name, a.balance, a.account_type, a.status
  FROM accounts a
  WHERE a.account_type = 'SAVINGS' AND a.balance >= 50000
  ORDER BY a.balance DESC
```

The key insight is that each object in the tree is strongly typed. `root.get("balance")` returns a `Path<BigDecimal>`, and `cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo()` expects compatible types. This catches type mismatches at compile time when used with the metamodel.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Account_; // Generated metamodel
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.criteria.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class TypeSafeSearchService {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public TypeSafeSearchService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }

    public List<Account> findHighBalanceSavings() {
        CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
        CriteriaQuery<Account> cq = cb.createQuery(Account.class);
        Root<Account> root = cq.from(Account.class);

        cq.where(
            cb.and(
                cb.equal(root.get(Account_.accountType), "SAVINGS"),
                cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get(Account_.balance),
                    new BigDecimal("100000.00"))
            )
        );

        return em.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
    }
}
```

### What is the JPA Metamodel?

The JPA Metamodel is a set of classes generated at compile time that provide type-safe field references. Instead of using string field names like `root.get("balance")`, you use `root.get(Account_.balance)`. If a field is renamed, the build breaks immediately instead of failing at runtime.

To generate the metamodel, add the Hibernate JPA Metamodel Generator to your build:

```xml
<!-- Maven dependency -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.hibernate.orm</groupId>
    <artifactId>hibernate-jpamodelgen</artifactId>
    <scope>provided</scope>
</dependency>
```

The generator creates a class like this:

```java
// Auto-generated: Account_.java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.metamodel.SingularAttribute;
import jakarta.persistence.metamodel.StaticMetamodel;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@StaticMetamodel(Account.class)
public abstract class Account_ {
    public static volatile SingularAttribute<Account, String> accountId;
    public static volatile SingularAttribute<Account, String> holderName;
    public static volatile SingularAttribute<Account, BigDecimal> balance;
    public static volatile SingularAttribute<Account, String> accountType;
    public static volatile SingularAttribute<Account, String> status;
}
```

Now you use `Account_.balance` instead of the string `"balance"`. If someone renames the field, the compiler catches it.

```java
// Without metamodel -- string, fails at runtime if field is renamed
root.get("balance")

// With metamodel -- type-safe, fails at compile time if field is renamed
root.get(Account_.balance)
```

### How is the Criteria API different from JPQL?

This comparison table captures the key trade-offs.

| Feature | Criteria API | JPQL |
|---|---|---|
| Query style | Programmatic (objects) | Declarative (string) |
| Type safety | Yes (with metamodel) | No (string, fails at runtime) |
| Dynamic queries | Excellent -- add predicates conditionally | Painful -- string concatenation |
| Readability | Verbose, harder to read | Concise, easy to read |
| IDE support | Auto-complete with metamodel | Limited (string) |
| Refactoring | Rename field, build breaks (metamodel) | Rename field, query silently breaks |
| Learning curve | Steeper | Familiar SQL-like syntax |
| Use case | Dynamic filters, search screens | Fixed queries, reports |

```java
// JPQL -- readable but fragile for dynamic queries
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.status = :status AND a.balance > :min")
List<Account> findActiveHighBalance(
    @Param("status") String status,
    @Param("min") BigDecimal min);
// What if status is optional? Need a separate query or ugly COALESCE trick

// Criteria/Specification -- verbose but dynamic and safe
Specification<Account> spec = Specification
    .where(AccountSpecs.hasStatus(status))   // skipped if null
    .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(min));   // skipped if null
repo.findAll(spec);
// Handles any combination of optional filters cleanly
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

- The repository must extend `JpaSpecificationExecutor<T>` to use `findAll(Specification)`.
- A specification returning `null` means "no filter" -- it is skipped during composition.
- Specifications compose with `and()`, `or()`, and `not()` -- these are static methods on `Specification`.
- Criteria queries support projections into DTOs using `cb.construct()`.
- Criteria queries support joins using `root.join()` and fetch joins using `root.fetch()`.
- The metamodel generator must be configured in the build tool to get compile-time safety.

```java
// DTO projection with Criteria API
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountSummary {
    private final String accountId;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    public AccountSummary(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    // getters
}
```

```java
CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<AccountSummary> cq = cb.createQuery(AccountSummary.class);
Root<Account> root = cq.from(Account.class);

cq.select(cb.construct(AccountSummary.class,
    root.get("accountId"),
    root.get("balance")));
cq.where(cb.equal(root.get("status"), "ACTIVE"));

List<AccountSummary> summaries = em.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
```

```text
Expected output:
[AccountSummary{SAV2001, 500000.00}, AccountSummary{CUR3001, 120000.00}]
```

### What are common mistakes?

**Mistake 1: Building dynamic JPQL with string concatenation.**

```java
// WRONG -- fragile, injection-risky, hard to maintain
public List<Account> search(String type, BigDecimal min) {
    String jpql = "SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE 1=1";
    if (type != null) {
        jpql += " AND a.accountType = '" + type + "'"; // SQL INJECTION!
    }
    if (min != null) {
        jpql += " AND a.balance >= " + min;
    }
    return em.createQuery(jpql, Account.class).getResultList();
}
```

```java
// CORRECT -- use Specification with parameterized predicates
Specification<Account> spec = Specification
    .where(AccountSpecs.hasType(type))
    .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(min));
return repo.findAll(spec);
// Parameters are bound safely, no injection risk
```

**Mistake 2: Using string field names instead of the metamodel.**

```java
// WRONG -- string "balance" is not checked at compile time
root.get("balance"); // if someone renames balance to accountBalance,
                      // this fails at RUNTIME, not at compile time

// CORRECT -- use the metamodel for compile-time safety
root.get(Account_.balance); // if field is renamed, build breaks immediately
```

**Mistake 3: Fetch joins inside specifications breaking pagination.**

```java
// WRONG -- fetch join with pagination causes Hibernate to load
// ALL rows into memory and paginate in-memory (HHH000104 warning)
public static Specification<Account> withTransactions() {
    return (root, query, cb) -> {
        root.fetch("transactions", JoinType.LEFT); // FETCH JOIN
        return null;
    };
}
repo.findAll(spec, PageRequest.of(0, 10)); // in-memory pagination!
```

```java
// CORRECT -- use fetch join only for non-paginated queries,
// or use a regular join and a separate query for the collection
public static Specification<Account> withTransactions() {
    return (root, query, cb) -> {
        // Only fetch when the query is not a count query (pagination)
        if (query.getResultType() != Long.class) {
            root.fetch("transactions", JoinType.LEFT);
        }
        return null;
    };
}
```

### What about joins and fetch joins in Criteria?

The Criteria API supports joins for filtering on related entities and fetch joins for eager loading.

```java
package com.bank.spec;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import jakarta.persistence.criteria.Join;
import jakarta.persistence.criteria.JoinType;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountSpecs {

    // Join: filter accounts that have a transaction above a threshold
    public static Specification<Account> hasTransactionAbove(BigDecimal threshold) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> {
            if (threshold == null) return null;
            Join<Account, Transaction> txn = root.join("transactions", JoinType.INNER);
            query.distinct(true); // avoid duplicates from the join
            return cb.greaterThan(txn.get("amount"), threshold);
        };
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.spec.AccountSpecs;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class TransactionSearchService {

    private final AccountRepository repo;

    public TransactionSearchService(AccountRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    public List<Account> findAccountsWithLargeTransactions() {
        Specification<Account> spec = Specification
            .where(AccountSpecs.hasStatus("ACTIVE"))
            .and(AccountSpecs.hasTransactionAbove(new BigDecimal("100000.00")));

        return repo.findAll(spec);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
[Account{SAV2001, Priya, 500000.00}]
// Only accounts with at least one transaction above 100,000
```

### When should you use Criteria / Specification?

Use Criteria or Specification when:

- The query has **optional filters** that change at runtime.
- You need **type-safe** queries with metamodel support.
- You want **composable, reusable** query fragments.
- The search screen has multiple optional parameters.

Do NOT use Criteria or Specification when:

- The query is **fixed** and known at compile time. Use JPQL or @NamedQuery instead.
- **Readability** is the priority. JPQL is much easier to read than Criteria code.
- The query is simple enough for a **derived query method** like `findByStatusAndAccountType()`.

At National Bank of India, Pankaj's rule is: fixed queries use @Query or @NamedQuery for readability, and dynamic search screens use Specification for flexibility. He never mixes the two for the same use case.

```java
// Fixed query -- use @Query for readability
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.status = 'ACTIVE' ORDER BY a.balance DESC")
List<Account> findAllActive();

// Dynamic search -- use Specification for flexibility
Specification<Account> spec = Specification
    .where(AccountSpecs.hasType(request.getType()))
    .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(request.getMinBalance()))
    .and(AccountSpecs.hasStatus(request.getStatus()))
    .and(AccountSpecs.holderNameContains(request.getName()));
repo.findAll(spec, request.getPageable());
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Specification testing.** Each specification is a pure function (root, query, cb -> predicate), so you can test it in isolation with a mocked CriteriaBuilder. In practice, integration tests with an in-memory database are more reliable.
- **Specification with pagination.** `JpaSpecificationExecutor.findAll(Specification, Pageable)` returns a `Page<T>`. Watch for the HHH000104 warning if you combine fetch joins with pagination.
- **Complex boolean logic.** Specifications support nested boolean expressions: `Specification.where(a.or(b)).and(c.or(d))`. This maps to `(A OR B) AND (C OR D)` in SQL.
- **Criteria for aggregation.** Use `cb.count()`, `cb.sum()`, `cb.avg()` with `cq.groupBy()` for aggregate queries. Return results as a DTO using `cb.construct()`.
- **Performance.** Criteria queries are compiled to the same SQL as equivalent JPQL. There is no performance difference at runtime. The overhead is in the verbosity of building the query in Java.
- **Alternative: Querydsl.** Some teams prefer Querydsl as a more readable alternative to the Criteria API. Querydsl generates Q-classes (similar to the metamodel) and provides a fluent API.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.spec.AccountSpecs;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.domain.Specification;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountPaginatedSearchService {

    private final AccountRepository repo;

    public AccountPaginatedSearchService(AccountRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public Page<Account> search(
            String type, BigDecimal minBalance, int page, int size) {

        Specification<Account> spec = Specification
            .where(AccountSpecs.hasType(type))
            .and(AccountSpecs.balanceAtLeast(minBalance))
            .and(AccountSpecs.hasStatus("ACTIVE"));

        PageRequest pageable = PageRequest.of(page, size,
            Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "balance"));

        return repo.findAll(spec, pageable);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (type="SAVINGS", minBalance=10000, page=0, size=10):
Page 0 of 1, total 3 elements:
[Account{SAV2001, Priya, 500000.00},
 Account{SAV2003, Rajesh, 150000.00},
 Account{SAV2005, Pankaj, 75000.00}]
```
